\\log x)^{-1}+O(\\log\\log x(\\log x)^{-2}$ for the primitive root conjecture.\nL39: This note proves an unconditional asymptotic formula $\\pi(u,x)=\\delta(u)x(\\log x)^{-1}+O(x(\\log x)^{-2}$ of the same result, where $\\delta(u)>0$ is the density constant. ^{†}^{†} August 24, 2026\nL40: Mathematics Subject Classifications: Primary 11A07, Secondary 11N37.\nL41: Keywords: Prime Number; Primitive Root; Artin Primitive Root Conjecture.\nL42: ###### Contents\nL43: \nL44:   1. cite6†1 Introduction L45:   2. cite7†2 Characteristic Functions in Finite Fields L46:   3. cite10†3 Finite Summation Kernel and Gauss Sum L47:   4. cite13†4 Estimates of Power Exponential Sums L48:   5. cite15†5 Evaluation of the Main Term L49:   6. cite16†6 Estimate for the Error Term L50:   7. cite17†7 Proof of Main Result L51:   8. cite18†8 Application To Repeated Decimals L52:   9. cite20†References L53: ## 1 Introduction\nL54: The symbols $\\mathbb{N}=\\{0,1,2,3,\\ldots\\}$, $\\mathbb{Z}=\\{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\ldots\\}$ and $\\mathbb{P}=\\{2,3,5,\\ldots\\}$ denote the set of natural numbers, the set integers and the set of prime numbers respectively. Let $x\\geq 1$ be a large real number and let $\\pi(x)=\\#\\{\\text{prime }p\\leq x\\}$ be the prime counting function. The multiplicative order of an element in a finite field $u\\in\\mathbb{F}_{p}$ is denoted by $\\ord_{p}u$, see cite22†Definition cite23†2.1 . The constant $\\delta(u)\\geq 0$ is the density\nL55:  | $$\\delta(u)=\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\#\\left\\{p\\in\\mathbb{P}:\\ord_{p}(u)=p-1\\right\\}}{\\pi(x)}$$  |  | (1.1)\nL56: \nL57: of the subset of primes $\\mathscr{P}_{u}=\\left\\{p\\in\\mathbb{P}:\\ord_{p}(u)=p-1\\right\\}$ with a fixed primitive root $u$ in the set of primes $\\mathbb{P}$. The expected number of primes $p\\leq x$ with a fixed primitive root $u\\tmod p$ has the asymptotic formula\nL58: \nL59:  | $$\\pi_{u}(x)=\\#\\left\\{p\\leqslant x:\\ord_{p}(u)=p-1\\right\\}=\\delta(u)\\li(x)+O\\left(x(\\log x)^{-2}\\right),$$  |  | (1.2)\nL60: where $\\li(x)$ is the logarithm integral, as $x\\rightarrow\\infty$.\nL61: A conditional proof of this result was achieved in [cite24†21 ], and simplified sketches of the proof appear in [cite25†29 , p. 8], and similar references. The determination of the constant $\\delta(u)\\geq 0$ for a fixed integer $u\\in\\mathbb{Z}$ is an interesting technical problem, [cite24†21 , p. 218], [cite26†26 ], [cite27†25 ], et alii. An introduction to its historical development, and its calculations is covered in [cite25†29 , pp. 3–10], and [cite28†41 ]. More recent results are given in [cite29†43 ], [cite30†37 ], et alia.\nL62: The Artin primitive root conjecture on average\nL63: \nL64:  | $$\\frac{1}{x}\\sum_{u\\leq x}\\pi_{u}(x)=a_{0}\\li(x)+O\\left(\\frac{x}{(\\log x)^{-c}}\\right).$$  |  | (1.3)\nL65: \nL66: The Artin constant is defined by\nL67: \nL68:  | $\\displaystyle a_{0}=\\sum_{n\\geq 1}\\frac{\\mu(n}{[\\mathcal{K}_{n}:\\mathbb{Q}]}=\\prod_{p\\geq 2}\\left(1-\\frac{1}{p(p-1)}\\right)$  |  | (1.4)\nL69: where $[\\mathcal{K}_{n}:\\mathbb{Q}]$ is a number field extension of $\\mathbb{Q}$ and $c>1$ is an arbitrary number, was proved in [cite28†41 ]. These works had shown that almost all admissible integers $u$ are primitive roots for infinitely many primes. The number of exceptions is a subset of zero density in $\\mathbb{Z}$. The individual quantity $\\pi_{u}(x)$ in (cite31†1.2 ) can be slightly different from the average quantity in (cite32†1.4 ).\nL70: The variations, discovered by the Lehmers using numerical experiments, depend on the primes decomposition of the fixed value $u$, see [cite24†21 , p. 220], [cite25†29 , p. 3], and similar references for the exact formula for the density $\\delta(u)\\geq 0$.\nL71: These results have been extended to quadratic numbers fields in [cite33†5 ], [NW2000], [cite34†38 ], and other number fields in [cite35†22 ], [cite36†1 ], [cite37†30 ], et cetera. Other related results are given in [cite38†31 ], [cite39†16 ], [cite40†36 ], [cite41†2 ], et alii. The Artin primitive root conjecture for functions fields was proved by Bilharz, see [cite42†39 , Theorem 10.11], and the same conjecture for polynomials over finite fields was proved in [cite43†35 ]. The literature on this topic is vast and extensive.\nL72: This note introduces a simple unconditional proof of the primitive root conjecture over the integers $\\mathbb{Z}$.\nL73: ###### Theorem 1.1.\nL74: \nL75: Afixed integer $u\\neq\\pm 1,v^{2}$ is a primitive root mod $p$ for infinitely many primes $p\\geq 2$. In addition, the density of these primes satisfies\nL76: \nL77:  | $$\\pi_{u}(x)=\\#\\left\\{p\\leq x:\\ord_{p}(u)=p-1\\right\\}=\\delta(u)\\li(x)+O\\left(x(\\log x)^{-2}\\right),$$  |  | (1.5)\nL78: \nL79: where $\\li(x)$ is the logarithm integral, and $\\delta(u)>0$ is a constant, for all large numbers $x\\geq 1$ unconditionally.\nL80: cite44†Section cite7†2 to cite45†Section cite16†6 introduce the notation and some standard results related to or applicable to the investigation of primitive roots in cyclic groups. cite46†Section cite17†7 presents a proof of cite47†Theorem cite48†1.1 .\nL81: ## 2 Characteristic Functions in Finite Fields\nL82: \nL83: The characteristic function $\\Psi:G\\longrightarrow\\{0,1\\}$ of primitive elements is one of the standard analytic tools employed to investigate the various properties of primitive roots in cyclic groups $G$. Many equivalent representations of the characteristic function $\\Psi$ of primitive elements are possible, a few are investigated here.\nL84: ###### Definition 2.1.\nL85: \nL86: The class function $\\ord:\\mathbb{F}_{p}^{\\times}\\longrightarrow\\{d<p:d\\mid p-1\\}$ defined by $\\ord_{p}u=\\min\\#\\{n<p:u^{n}\\equiv 1\\bmod p\\}$ measures the multiplicative order of an element $u\\neq 0$ in the multiplicative group $\\mathbb{F}_{p}^{\\times}$. An element of maximal multiplicative order $\\ord_{p}u=p-1$ is called a primitive element.\nL87: ### 2.1 Divisor Dependent Characteristic Function\nL88: \nL89: The divisor-dependent representation of the characteristic function detects the multiplicative order of an element by means of the divisors of the totient function $\\varphi(p)=p-1$. The version for primitive elements in the prime finite fields $\\mathbb{F}_{p}$ is described below.\nL90: ###### Lemma 2.1.\nL91: \nL92: Let $p\\geq 2$ be an integer and let $\\chi$ be a multiplicative character of order $\\ord\\chi=d$. If $u\\in\\mathbb{F}_{p}$ is a nonzero element, then\nL93: \nL94:  | $$\\Psi(u)=\\frac{\\varphi(p-1)}{p-1}\\sum_{d\\mid\\varphi(p)}\\frac{\\mu(d)}{\\varphi(q)}\\sum_{\\ord\\chi=d}\\chi(u)=\\left\\{\\begin{array}[]{ll}1&\\text{ if }\\ord_{p}(u)=p-1,\\\\\nL95: 0&\\text{ if }\\ord_{p}(u)\\neq p-1,\\\\\nL96: \\end{array}\\right.$$  |\nL97: \nL98: where $\\mu:\\mathbb{N}\\longrightarrow\\{-1,0,1\\}$ is the Mobius function.\nL99: There are a few other variant proofs of this result, these are widely available in the literature. Almost every result in the theory of primitive roots in finite fields is based on this characteristic function, but sometimes written in different forms. This technique was developed by Landau, Vinogradov, Davenport, Erdos, and other authors, see [cite49†24 ], [cite50†44 ], [cite51†6 ], [cite52†12 ], [cite53†47 ], et alii.\nL100: An extension of this characteristic function to the finite ring $\\mathbb{Z}/n\\mathbb{Z}$ is presented in [cite54†28 , Lemma 4]. The main obstacle in this technique is the primes decomposition of the totients $p-1$. Specifically, the finite sum $\\sum_{d\\mid p-1}|\\mu(d)|\\ll p^{\\varepsilon}$ is an obstacle in many applications.\nL101: ### 2.2 Divisorfree Characteristic Functions\nL102: \nL103: A new divisorfree representation of the characteristic function of primitive element is introduced in this section. It detects the order $\\text{ord}_{p}(u)\\geq 1$ of the element $u\\in\\mathbb{F}_{p}$ by means of the solutions of the equation $\\tau^{s}-u=0$ in $\\mathbb{F}_{p}$, where $u,\\tau$ are constants, and $s\\in\\mathscr{R}=\\{s<p:\\gcd(s,p-1)=1\\}$ is a variable.\nL104: ###### Lemma 2.2.\nL105: \nL106: Let $p\\geq 2$ be a prime and let $\\tau$ be a primitive root mod $p$ and let $\\psi\\neq 1$ be a nonprincipal additive character of order $\\ord\\psi=p$. If $u\\in\\mathbb{F}_{p}$ is a nonzero element, then\nL107: \nL108:  | $$\\Psi(u)=\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}1\\leq s\\leq p-1\\\\\nL109: \\gcd(s,p-1)=1\\end{subarray}}\\frac{1}{p}\\sum_{0\\leq t\\leq p-1}\\psi\\left((\\tau^{s}-u)t\\right)=\\left\\{\\begin{array}[]{ll}1&\\text{ if }\\ord_{p}(u)=p-1,\\\\\nL110: 0&\\text{ if }\\ord_{p}(u)\\neq p-1.\\\\\nL111: \\end{array}\\right.$$  |\nL112: ###### Proof.\nL113: \nL114: Set the additive character $\\psi(s)=e^{i2\\pi as/p}\\in\\mathbb{C}$. Now, as the index $s\\in\\mathscr{R}$ ranges over the integers relatively prime to $\\varphi(p)=p-1$, the element $\\tau^{n}\\in\\mathbb{F}_{p}^{\\times}$ ranges over the primitive roots modulo $p$. Accordingly, the equation\nL115: \nL116:  | $\\displaystyle a=\\tau^{n}-u=0$  |  | (2.1)\nL117: \nL118: has a unique solution $s\\in\\mathscr{R}$ if and only if the fixed element $u\\in\\mathbb{F}_{p}$ is a primitive root. This implies that the inner sum in\nL119:  | $$\\sum_{\\begin{subarray}{c}1\\leq s\\leq p-1\\\\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - 26--PPH-OP-2404005-ANT19 - Corrected 25-07-2024 (https://pphmjopenaccess.com/index.php/jpjana/article/download/1620/1259/5029)\nciteturn7view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 11; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"id\":37}); Total lines: 347\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.ejpam.com/ejpam/article/view/3182/585)\nciteturn7view2 [w